# What CI requires `loom-exec --self-test` to have actually enforced.
#
# The sandbox reports honestly: a probe whose kernel layer the machine
# cannot provide prints SKIPPED with a reason and does not fail the run
# (packages/sandbox/CLAUDE.md, "Degraded means degraded, out loud"). That
# is the right behaviour for a developer's laptop and the wrong one for a
# merge gate, where a run that enforced nothing would be indistinguishable
# from a run that enforced everything. This file closes that gap: it is
# the declared, reviewed answer to "which layers must this CI machine
# really have applied", and `.github/scripts/enforcement_report.sh` fails
# the job when the run and this file disagree in either direction.
#
# Format, one probe per line:
#
#   required|<probe name>
#   known-gap|<probe name>|<why this machine cannot provide the layer>
#
# Rules the checker enforces:
#
#   - a `required` probe that did not print ENFORCED fails the job;
#   - a `known-gap` probe that *did* print ENFORCED also fails the job,
#     because the gap is over and this file has started lying;
#   - a probe in the run but not in this file, or in this file but not in
#     the run, fails the job: the probe set changed and nobody looked.
#
# Moving a probe from `required` to `known-gap` is the only way to make a
# missing layer stop failing CI, it takes a written reason, and it shows
# up in review as what it is — a deliberate reduction in what CI proves.
# Deleting a line is never the fix.

# --- Loom-side layers: these need no kernel opt-in and hold everywhere,
# --- including inside a stripped container. Never movable.
required|env not in allowlist withheld
required|output flood truncated at cap
required|orphaned grandchild reaped via pgroup

# --- Kernel layers. The jail job installs bubblewrap and lifts Ubuntu's
# --- AppArmor restriction on unprivileged user namespaces before running
# --- the self-test, so these are required of a hosted Linux runner.
required|write outside writable_roots denied
required|protected path write denied
required|direct socket denied under network off

# --- cgroup v2.
#
# `internal/cgroup.Detect` returns the helper's *own* cgroup as the base,
# and `Setup` then creates the per-exec cgroup inside it and writes
# `memory.max`/`pids.max` there. Those files only exist if the base cgroup
# lists the controllers in `cgroup.subtree_control`, and cgroup v2's
# no-internal-process rule forbids a non-root cgroup from having both
# member processes and enabled controllers for its children. A helper
# therefore has a delegated hierarchy it can use only when it runs in the
# true root cgroup, which is exempt from that rule — not the shape a
# GitHub-hosted runner (or any systemd host) puts a workflow step in.
#
# The jail job records this rather than papering over it: it runs the
# self-test in the production shape, where this probe is expected to skip,
# and then runs a clearly-labelled supplementary probe from the root
# cgroup whose result is printed but never allowed to satisfy this file.
known-gap|fork bomb capped by pids limit|the runner's workflow step lives in a systemd service cgroup, and per-exec cgroups can only be created from the root cgroup (cgroup v2 no-internal-process rule); the supplementary root-cgroup run in the jail job reports what this kernel would give
